WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.100 --> 00:00:07.950
PROFESSOR: I don't have
anything very structured,

00:00:07.950 --> 00:00:11.800
but I just want to get
the conversation going.

00:00:11.800 --> 00:00:15.450
The first question I
have for you guys is,

00:00:15.450 --> 00:00:18.190
tell me about a time you
had to problem solve.

00:00:18.190 --> 00:00:21.990
So I want you to talk to
the person next to you

00:00:21.990 --> 00:00:27.670
and discuss when you had
a problem solve today.

00:00:27.670 --> 00:00:31.820
[SIDE CONVERSATIONS]

00:00:31.820 --> 00:00:33.045
Girld who--

00:00:33.045 --> 00:00:34.510
AUDIENCE: Build.

00:00:34.510 --> 00:00:36.700
PROFESSOR: Do I
have any volunteers

00:00:36.700 --> 00:00:38.280
for some good examples?

00:00:38.280 --> 00:00:41.118
How about on the rest
where-- OK, go ahead.

00:00:41.118 --> 00:00:41.993
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

00:00:47.870 --> 00:00:49.560
PROFESSOR: What'd you do to--

00:00:49.560 --> 00:00:52.150
AUDIENCE: Well you have to
figure out where exactly you

00:00:52.150 --> 00:00:54.430
went wrong so you can fix it.

00:00:54.430 --> 00:00:56.340
PROFESSOR: Nice.

00:00:56.340 --> 00:00:57.175
What about you?

00:00:57.175 --> 00:00:59.720
AUDIENCE: It's not very
like an intricate problem,

00:00:59.720 --> 00:01:02.480
but everybody in my
family has an iPhone.

00:01:02.480 --> 00:01:07.110
We all take each other's
chargers if we lose our own.

00:01:07.110 --> 00:01:10.140
So to solve that
problem I got a Sharpie

00:01:10.140 --> 00:01:12.287
and I drew a heart
on my charger.

00:01:12.287 --> 00:01:13.120
PROFESSOR: Oh, nice.

00:01:13.120 --> 00:01:15.880
AUDIENCE: And I
knew this is mine.

00:01:15.880 --> 00:01:17.233
And I couldn't take it anymore.

00:01:17.233 --> 00:01:19.500
[INAUDIBLE]

00:01:19.500 --> 00:01:21.570
PROFESSOR: That's a good idea.

00:01:21.570 --> 00:01:22.790
Anyone else?

00:01:22.790 --> 00:01:27.580
Anyone-- how about for the
raspberry pie activity?

00:01:27.580 --> 00:01:29.212
Did everything work flawlessly?

00:01:29.212 --> 00:01:30.170
AUDIENCE: Probably not.

00:01:30.170 --> 00:01:31.170
PROFESSOR: Yeah?

00:01:31.170 --> 00:01:33.670
AUDIENCE: If I brought
that picture of the camera

00:01:33.670 --> 00:01:37.080
up on the camera it was
upside down and backwards.

00:01:37.080 --> 00:01:39.080
We figured out how to
flip it and make it right.

00:01:39.080 --> 00:01:39.913
PROFESSOR: Oh, nice.

00:01:39.913 --> 00:01:44.030
Was it hard figuring out where
to put that line of code?

00:01:44.030 --> 00:01:44.780
AUDIENCE: Kind of.

00:01:44.780 --> 00:01:48.810
We figured out which section
was brought up on the camera

00:01:48.810 --> 00:01:51.334
and which section was
brought on the computer

00:01:51.334 --> 00:01:52.500
and put it in the right one.

00:01:52.500 --> 00:01:53.340
PROFESSOR: Good.

00:01:53.340 --> 00:01:56.720
How about for processing?

00:01:56.720 --> 00:01:58.365
Any good examples?

00:01:58.365 --> 00:01:59.912
Yeah, in the back.

00:01:59.912 --> 00:02:03.054
AUDIENCE: I had to figure
out what the camera was

00:02:03.054 --> 00:02:06.072
trying to see, so--

00:02:06.072 --> 00:02:07.613
PROFESSOR: How'd
you figure that out?

00:02:07.613 --> 00:02:08.738
AUDIENCE: Well, [INAUDIBLE]

00:02:14.520 --> 00:02:16.880
PROFESSOR: Great.

00:02:16.880 --> 00:02:20.670
My next question is,
how would you improve

00:02:20.670 --> 00:02:22.650
the camera you built today?

00:02:22.650 --> 00:02:26.840
So this time turn to someone
behind you or in front of you,

00:02:26.840 --> 00:02:29.490
and tell them what you
would do differently.

00:02:29.490 --> 00:02:34.254
[SIDE CONVERSATIONS]

00:02:34.254 --> 00:02:35.170
PROFESSOR: Girls who--

00:02:35.170 --> 00:02:36.740
AUDIENCE: Build.

00:02:36.740 --> 00:02:41.310
PROFESSOR: Do I have any
volunteers from the second row?

00:02:41.310 --> 00:02:41.810
Yes?

00:02:41.810 --> 00:02:44.184
AUDIENCE: We said that we'd
make the camera more durable,

00:02:44.184 --> 00:02:46.010
because ours broke
and so did theirs.

00:02:46.010 --> 00:02:48.826
Like the plastic part
where you hold it together.

00:02:48.826 --> 00:02:49.990
PROFESSOR: Nice.

00:02:49.990 --> 00:02:52.244
What kind of material do
you think you would use?

00:02:52.244 --> 00:02:55.550
AUDIENCE: We like metal
or a better plastic.

00:02:55.550 --> 00:02:58.540
PROFESSOR: Did any of you
guys play with Alex's lenses?

00:02:58.540 --> 00:03:00.684
Did you see how
protective they were?

00:03:00.684 --> 00:03:02.350
And a lot of professional
photographers,

00:03:02.350 --> 00:03:04.150
they break them all the time.

00:03:04.150 --> 00:03:08.710
So-- yeah there are ways
to make it more structured

00:03:08.710 --> 00:03:09.970
and prevent it from breaking.

00:03:09.970 --> 00:03:13.380
Any other ideas
besides the case?

00:03:13.380 --> 00:03:14.616
Yeah?

00:03:14.616 --> 00:03:15.582
AUDIENCE: The speed.

00:03:15.582 --> 00:03:17.165
It would take a photo
a couple seconds

00:03:17.165 --> 00:03:19.450
after you pressed the button.

00:03:19.450 --> 00:03:20.410
PROFESSOR: Yeah.

00:03:20.410 --> 00:03:22.368
AUDIENCE: So it would be
a lot easier to photos

00:03:22.368 --> 00:03:26.300
if the moment you see
the image you want,

00:03:26.300 --> 00:03:31.136
if you took the photo it would
get that exact moment instead

00:03:31.136 --> 00:03:32.760
of like two seconds
after of something.

00:03:32.760 --> 00:03:33.426
PROFESSOR: Yeah.

00:03:33.426 --> 00:03:35.970
That's a really good point.

00:03:35.970 --> 00:03:36.733
Anyone else?

00:03:40.070 --> 00:03:42.180
Yeah?

00:03:42.180 --> 00:03:44.981
AUDIENCE: I tried to
make it so that when

00:03:44.981 --> 00:03:46.936
you are zooming the
camera around and looking

00:03:46.936 --> 00:03:51.336
at the image on
the screen it moves

00:03:51.336 --> 00:03:56.820
more smoothly, so that it's not
jerky images as you're moving.

00:03:59.730 --> 00:04:02.040
PROFESSOR: Do you guys
remember how in the code

00:04:02.040 --> 00:04:05.360
it said-- you had code
for displaying that image,

00:04:05.360 --> 00:04:10.710
and then you had code for
displaying the-- or capturing

00:04:10.710 --> 00:04:13.010
the picture that you took
when you pressed the button?

00:04:13.010 --> 00:04:15.780
So when you have that code
for capturing the image,

00:04:15.780 --> 00:04:17.209
when it's displaying
on the screen

00:04:17.209 --> 00:04:20.860
it's actually taking that image
and then displaying it to you.

00:04:20.860 --> 00:04:23.310
So that's a lot of
work for a computer

00:04:23.310 --> 00:04:27.080
to do versus just-- I
think Gavin actually

00:04:27.080 --> 00:04:28.305
had looked at that.

00:04:28.305 --> 00:04:28.930
AUDIENCE: Yeah.

00:04:28.930 --> 00:04:30.340
I was trying to make it faster.

00:04:30.340 --> 00:04:31.673
PROFESSOR: What were your ideas?

00:04:31.673 --> 00:04:32.834
What would you have done?

00:04:32.834 --> 00:04:35.672
AUDIENCE: So there's a
way that you can just

00:04:35.672 --> 00:04:38.077
write some memory
instead of-- so there's

00:04:38.077 --> 00:04:39.160
different types of memory.

00:04:39.160 --> 00:04:42.540
There's RAM versus your
permanent storage on the SD

00:04:42.540 --> 00:04:43.040
card.

00:04:43.040 --> 00:04:45.040
The SD card is a lot slower.

00:04:45.040 --> 00:04:46.989
So you can save it
into your local memory

00:04:46.989 --> 00:04:49.887
and cache it, and
then throw that one

00:04:49.887 --> 00:04:52.269
the screen without interacting
with the file system.

00:04:52.269 --> 00:04:53.185
That should be faster.

00:04:53.185 --> 00:04:54.370
But I didn't get to it.

00:04:54.370 --> 00:04:55.637
PROFESSOR: Sweet.

00:04:55.637 --> 00:04:57.720
The volunteers are thinking
about this stuff, too.

00:05:01.830 --> 00:05:04.140
The last question
I have for you is,

00:05:04.140 --> 00:05:06.170
tell me about an
Instagram filter

00:05:06.170 --> 00:05:09.280
that you changed from the
original instructions.

00:05:09.280 --> 00:05:12.161
So this time turn to
someone next to you,

00:05:12.161 --> 00:05:14.369
but someone different that
you haven't talked to yet.

00:05:14.369 --> 00:05:19.060
[SIDE CONVERSATIONS]

00:05:19.060 --> 00:05:19.923
Girls who--

00:05:19.923 --> 00:05:22.207
AUDIENCE: Build.

00:05:22.207 --> 00:05:22.790
PROFESSOR: OK.

00:05:22.790 --> 00:05:25.410
How about from the third row.

00:05:25.410 --> 00:05:27.618
What did you guys change
with your filters?

00:05:30.610 --> 00:05:31.870
Any volunteers?

00:05:31.870 --> 00:05:34.840
Did you guys just keep it
the same the whole time?

00:05:34.840 --> 00:05:35.340
No?

00:05:35.340 --> 00:05:36.827
What'd you do?

00:05:36.827 --> 00:05:37.410
AUDIENCE: Oh--

00:05:37.410 --> 00:05:38.035
AUDIENCE: Wait.

00:05:38.035 --> 00:05:39.380
PROFESSOR: Oh.

00:05:39.380 --> 00:05:42.024
You can both go.

00:05:42.024 --> 00:05:43.016
AUDIENCE: Oh.

00:05:43.016 --> 00:05:45.000
I did one where
you could click it,

00:05:45.000 --> 00:05:47.976
and then I just put
on the [INAUDIBLE]

00:05:47.976 --> 00:05:49.480
PROFESSOR: Oh, nice.

00:05:49.480 --> 00:05:53.710
Did anyone else do the
image effects change?

00:05:53.710 --> 00:05:54.210
Oh, nice.

00:05:54.210 --> 00:05:57.117
One person back there.

00:05:57.117 --> 00:05:58.200
What about from over here?

00:05:58.200 --> 00:06:01.060
What did you guys do with
the Instagram filters?

00:06:01.060 --> 00:06:02.060
Yeah?

00:06:02.060 --> 00:06:05.560
AUDIENCE: Well, [INAUDIBLE]
the original instructions.

00:06:05.560 --> 00:06:07.816
We changed the colors
on the first one

00:06:07.816 --> 00:06:09.690
from a red-ish, greenish
color to a lavender.

00:06:09.690 --> 00:06:10.980
PROFESSOR: Oh, neat.

00:06:10.980 --> 00:06:11.660
Cool.

00:06:11.660 --> 00:06:14.045
The RGB values?

00:06:14.045 --> 00:06:14.670
AUDIENCE: Yeah.

00:06:14.670 --> 00:06:14.900
PROFESSOR: Yeah?

00:06:14.900 --> 00:06:15.400
You, too?

00:06:15.400 --> 00:06:17.192
AUDIENCE: I messed with
the turn everything

00:06:17.192 --> 00:06:19.275
except the color you clicked
on into grayscale one

00:06:19.275 --> 00:06:20.970
to turn it into--
turn just the color

00:06:20.970 --> 00:06:22.900
you clicked on into black.

00:06:22.900 --> 00:06:24.900
PROFESSOR: Oh, nice.

00:06:24.900 --> 00:06:29.290
Those are all
really great ideas.

00:06:29.290 --> 00:06:32.670
So this concludes the program.

00:06:32.670 --> 00:06:35.070
I hope you guys had
a wonderful time

00:06:35.070 --> 00:06:38.100
and learned about things
that you wouldn't normally

00:06:38.100 --> 00:06:40.790
think about when you
think of computer coding

00:06:40.790 --> 00:06:42.950
and engineering.

00:06:42.950 --> 00:06:46.682
There were a ton of people-- all
the volunteers in the morning,

00:06:46.682 --> 00:06:48.140
and then the ones
in the afternoon,

00:06:48.140 --> 00:06:49.700
and our keynote speakers.

00:06:49.700 --> 00:06:52.140
So they are in the
room next door,

00:06:52.140 --> 00:06:56.120
but let's give them a round of
applause so they can hear you.

00:06:56.120 --> 00:06:59.330
[APPLAUSE]

00:06:59.330 --> 00:07:02.320
And you guys probably caught
on to this, but most of us

00:07:02.320 --> 00:07:04.760
work at MIT Lincoln Laboratory.

00:07:04.760 --> 00:07:06.630
I do ocean robotics there.

00:07:06.630 --> 00:07:09.680
And I hope you got a chance
to talk to your volunteers

00:07:09.680 --> 00:07:12.410
and hear about what
they do at the lab.

00:07:12.410 --> 00:07:14.350
We do all sorts of things.

00:07:14.350 --> 00:07:17.060
And SolidWorks also
sponsored this.

00:07:17.060 --> 00:07:21.170
And MIT OpenCourseWare-- have
any of you guys heard of that?

00:07:21.170 --> 00:07:23.290
Raise your hand if you have.

00:07:23.290 --> 00:07:24.520
Oh, not too many of you.

00:07:24.520 --> 00:07:25.100
Good.

00:07:25.100 --> 00:07:28.710
So MIT OpenCourseWare
is online curriculum.

00:07:28.710 --> 00:07:32.180
So if you guys want
to get this code,

00:07:32.180 --> 00:07:36.560
we're publishing it online
so you can play more with it

00:07:36.560 --> 00:07:40.710
and get the software and
all the instructions.

00:07:40.710 --> 00:07:43.130
We also have a curriculum
and wearable technology.

00:07:43.130 --> 00:07:45.070
So you can check that out, too.

00:07:45.070 --> 00:07:50.270
And all this information will
be sent to you in an e-mail.

00:07:50.270 --> 00:07:53.250
And we also have
a Facebook page,

00:07:53.250 --> 00:07:56.950
it's just Girls Who Build,
and a Twitter account.

00:07:56.950 --> 00:07:59.800
And I'll be posting all
the material and photos

00:07:59.800 --> 00:08:04.150
from this workshop
on those websites.

00:08:04.150 --> 00:08:08.630
So with that, I have a bunch
of fun demos for you guys.

00:08:08.630 --> 00:08:14.930
We have 3D scanning,
and photogrammetry,

00:08:14.930 --> 00:08:19.620
and a light field camera
in the research area.

00:08:19.620 --> 00:08:24.860
So you can rotate
around the stations

00:08:24.860 --> 00:08:28.840
and ask questions and
talk to the volunteers.

00:08:28.840 --> 00:08:32.409
We also have the
computer still set up,

00:08:32.409 --> 00:08:34.700
so you have a little bit
of time to keep playing

00:08:34.700 --> 00:08:36.679
with the code and the cameras.

00:08:36.679 --> 00:08:40.620
So in conclusion, thank
you all for coming.

00:08:40.620 --> 00:08:44.680
And feel free to come
up to me and ask me

00:08:44.680 --> 00:08:46.170
more about camera technology.

00:08:46.170 --> 00:08:49.880
We'd happy to tell you about
other fun projects you can do.

00:08:49.880 --> 00:08:51.270
Thank you, guys.

00:08:51.270 --> 00:08:52.820
[APPLAUSE]

